\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\title{States, flows and coordinates}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The state is a point #{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}. The vector field is a
map #{f}, and the evolution is the equation #{\dot x=f(x)}. The flow
is the family #{\varphi^{t}}, also written #{\varphi_{t}}.
[Dynamical system](fnip-0004) fixes both glyphs: Kuznetsov writes the
superscript, and the subscript is the same family.}

\p{A statement names its state space, its vector field, and its
parameters before it uses them. An argument that uses coordinates
first names those coordinates, and the order is part of the naming.
The pair #{(V,n)} and the pair #{(n,V)} are different identifications
of the same two quantities with #{ \mathbb{R}^{2} }.}

\p{In the running example the coordinates are the book's pair
#{(V,n)}, in that order, as in
\ref{fnip-0005}. A voltage trace is the coordinate
#{V} of a point on a [trajectory](fnip-0009).
\ref{fnip-0010} separates that
function of time from the pair #{(V(t),n(t))}.}

\p{An example that holds parameters fixed names them once and names the
column of Table 3.1 from which they come.
\citet{Table 3.1, p. 51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
prints three columns, Hopf, SNLC, and homoclinic. A number taken from
one column is not reused under the name of another. The Hopf column is
the column of the Morris–Lecar examples in
[State variables and phase space](fnip-0002).}
